// docs/spec_revision/archive/measurements/restructure_survey.vsm — MEASUREMENT
// for rmw3's four-step restructure proposal (2026-08-10). Measured before
// evaluated, which is the discipline he named the same day: "antes de actuar,
// medir".
// ARCHIVED 2026-08-17 (ARCHIVE-POLICY): survey cumplido, acciones absorbidas
// por revision_plan; DATED RECORD — citado por linage, ¬autoridad.
// ∄ move is proposed here. This establishes WHAT IS, so the proposal can be
// judged against numbers instead of against intuition.

survey(RESTRUCT-SURVEY-v1) {

  // ══ 1 · WHAT MUST LOAD IN boot/ ═══════════════════════════════════════
  boot_closure: {
    test: "boot/ is what you hand an EXTERNAL agent. So the test its purpose
           imposes: given ONLY boot/, does every identifier boot/ cites
           resolve?"
    result: "✗ NO. boot/ cites 59 identifiers and declares 66, but 18 of what
            it cites are declared NOWHERE in boot/."
    who_leaks: "vsl_language 17 ▪ regulation_layer 1"
    where_they_live: "16 of 18 in theory/ — mostly dynamics_choice
            (Ax_RecurrentSet, Ax_Ω_Closure, Ax_ψ_Preserve, Def_Adaptiveτ,
            Def_SeqLockσ, Def_Filtration_L, Def_RecertEpoch, Def_ArbiterAPI,
            Def_Reframe_Staged, Ax_InterfaceOnly) ▪ vos_core_theory (A8.vOS)
            ▪ vos_proofs (Cor_4)"
    reading: "the leak is ONE-SIDED and it is vsl_language's. The primer,
            covenant, hard_rules and communication_protocol are closed. So the
            question is ¬which documents leave boot/ — it is whether
            vsl_language's notation section should CITE the dynamics or
            DECLARE what it needs. Trimming vsl_language and promoting
            dynamics_choice are opposite fixes and the choice is rmw3's."
    // BOOT-NOT-CLOSED — CLOSED 2026-08-10. rmw3 ruled TRIM. The 18 was ¬real:
    // two index bugs of mine (a formula-valued declaration read as prose, and
    // Greek missing from the identifier charset) made 14 DECLARATIONS look
    // like citations. 18 → 4, then 4 → 0 by touching four lines:
    //   Ax_InterfaceOnly gained ≜ — its siblings state a RELATION and it
    //     stated a bare predicate, so boot/ read as leaking its OWN axiom.
    //     A fix to the DOCUMENT, ¬another widening of the instrument.
    //   A8.vOS ▪ Cor_4 ▪ Def_ArbiterAPI were live EXAMPLES and forward
    //     references pointing out of boot/. An illustration must ¬create a
    //     dependency the boot set cannot resolve alone.
    // boot/ now satisfies its own test: hand it to an external agent with ∄
    // other context and every identifier it cites resolves inside it.
  }

  // ══ 2 · UNIVERSAL vs INSTANCE ════════════════════════════════════════
  substrate_split: {
    measure: "a document is INSTANCE-BOUND if it names a substrate artifact:
            LXC id ▪ 10.10.x ▪ n01/n02/n03 ▪ /dev/ ▪ a .service unit ▪ a VLAN
            ▪ a port. Agnostic if it names none."
    result: "18 agnostic ▪ 30 instance-bound, of 48."
    by_directory: "
            boot                6 agnostic ▪ 0 instance   ← already clean
            instance/templates 2 + s1-operations/s1-template 2 ▪ 0  ← absorbed 2026-08-14
            instance            1 ▪ 7                     ← already clean
            s1..s5 + s3star     0 ▪ 7                     ← ALL instance-bound
            infra ▪ plan        0 ▪ 2
            spec                2 ▪ 2
            theory              3 ▪ 10                    ← MIXED"
    finding: "rmw3 said theory/ fits ¬either category. The measurement says
            something sharper: theory/ is MOSTLY INSTANCE-BOUND (10 of 13).
            vos_core_theory carries 15 substrate references, symbiogenesis_lab
            12. So theory/ is ¬a third category standing above the split — it
            is a directory that has ¬been split yet.
            And the S1..S5 directories are 7/7 instance-bound: they describe
            THIS system, ¬the framework. Under the universal/instance cut they
            belong on the instance side WHOLE."
    // debt(THEORY-NOT-SPLIT, class=doc, owner=rmw3, gate=canon_decision)
    // theory/ measures 3 agnostic against 10 instance-bound, so it is ¬a third
    // category above the universal/instance cut — it is a directory that has ∄
    // been split. vos_core_theory carries 15 substrate references,
    // symbiogenesis_lab 12. Splitting it is the prerequisite for treating
    // theory/ as a sibling of system/ rather than a child.
  }

  // ══ 3 · WHAT REMAINS IN docs/ ════════════════════════════════════════
  unintegrated: {
    measure: "files under docs/ (excluding spec_revision, papers, sessions)
            named by NEITHER the compression ledger NOR the revision plan."
    result: "456 files ▪ 296 unnamed."
    concentration: "findings 23 ▪ deploy 16 ▪ internal 14 ▪ spec 14 ▪
            evaluation 12 ▪ rubric 10 ▪ opsroom agents 10 ▪
            experiments/vsl_compression 9 ▪ governance 9 ▪ vsf-bbh 9"
    caution: "296 is ¬a backlog of 296 documents to fuse. Most are DATED
            RECORDS — findings, deploy reports, rubric answers — which the
            plan's own principle says are cited as records, ¬promoted. The
            number to extract is: how many are LIVE SPECIFICATIONS that
            system/ silently lacks. That takes reading, ¬this measurement."
    // debt(DOCS-INTEGRATION-UNSURVEYED, class=doc, owner=rmw3, gate=none)
    // 296 files under docs/ are named by neither the ledger nor the plan.
    // That is ¬a fuse backlog: most are DATED RECORDS, which the plan cites
    // rather than promotes. The number that matters is how many are LIVE
    // SPECIFICATIONS system/ silently lacks, and separating those from records
    // is reading, ¬measurement.
  }

  // ══ 4 · KERNEL BLIND SPOTS ═══════════════════════════════════════════
  kernel_blind: {
    measure: "which .rs modules and pub consts of the kernel are NEVER named
            in system/."
    modules: "47 total ▪ 32 cited ▪ 15 BLIND, ≈5600 LOC:
            telemetry_canonical 693 ▪ coupling_registry 658 ▪
            learned_modes 535 ▪ invariant_checker 527 ▪ s5_covenant 472 ▪
            vmem 427 ▪ beacon 418 ▪ observability 380 ▪ supervisor 380 ▪
            s5_approval_nats 364 ▪ bbh_subscriber 268 ▪ bbs_embedded 213 ▪
            config 159 ▪ s3_star_audit 145 ▪ worker_proc 33"
    constants: "131 pub const ▪ 107 NEVER cited in system/ (82%)."
    why_this_is_the_largest: "R_param_resolution was ratified 2026-08-09 to
            classify every value as substrate-agnostic (canon) or instance
            artifact. 107 of the kernel's 131 constants are ¬even VISIBLE to
            the spec, so they have been classified as neither. The rule exists
            and 82% of its object is outside the room.
            s5_covenant.rs and invariant_checker.rs are the sharp ones: the
            covenant and the invariants are CONSTITUTIONAL, and their
            implementations are undocumented in the corpus that governs them."
    // debt(KERNEL-BLIND-TO-SPEC, class=kernel, owner=rmw3, gate=none)
    // 15 of 47 kernel modules (≈5600 LOC) and 107 of 131 pub const are never
    // named in system/. R_param_resolution classifies every value as canon or
    // instance, and 82% of its object is invisible to the spec — classified as
    // neither. s5_covenant.rs and invariant_checker.rs are the sharp ones: the
    // covenant and the invariants are constitutional, and their implementations
    // are undocumented in the corpus that governs them.
    // A blind module may be DEAD (bbs_embedded — BBS decommissioned 2026-08-02)
    // or undocumented; the measurement cannot tell them apart.
  }

  what_this_does_not_say: "
    ∄ recommendation to move anything. A blind module may be blind because it
    is DEAD (bbs_embedded — BBS was decommissioned 2026-08-02) or because it
    is undocumented, and this measurement cannot tell them apart. Neither can
    it tell a live spec in docs/ from a dated record. Both distinctions are
    READING, and reading is the ~70% ∄ instrument covers."
}

